TITLE Test B -- does decoupling H2 (Hdg) stop the carbon runaway?
#
# PURPOSE
#   OPTION3_decision.md sec 2 proposes that the reason all released organic
#   carbon ends up as CH4 is that the large dissolved-H2 reservoir sets
#   pe ~ -11.5 and acts as the electron donor. If H2 is entered as the
#   DECOUPLED element Hdg instead of coupled H(0)/H2, it can no longer donate
#   electrons, so oxidised carbon should stay oxidised.
#
#   This is the decisive cheap test of that hypothesis. It is worth running
#   BEFORE any module or config changes, because if it fails the whole
#   carbon-decoupling design has to change (fallback = add a decoupled
#   oxidised-carbon species, with the calcite caveat in sec 2).
#
# WHAT SUCCESS LOOKS LIKE
#   Simulation 1 (coupled H2):   C(-4) >> C(+4)   -- reproduces the artefact
#   Simulation 2 (decoupled Hdg): C(+4) retained  -- carbon stays oxidised
#
#   The number to compare against is from this project's own earlier output
#   (k1.00e-07_d194200_orb12): at pe -11.5, C(-4)/C(+4) = 3.1e-16 / 7.9e-33,
#   i.e. ~17 orders of magnitude in favour of methane. Simulation 1 should
#   reproduce something like that ratio. Simulation 2 should not.
#
# IF IT PARTLY WORKS
#   Watch pe in simulation 2. Decoupling H2 removes one electron donor but
#   the Fe(II) minerals are still there. If pe stays very low and carbon
#   still reduces, the hypothesis is wrong in an informative way: the mineral
#   assemblage, not H2, is setting pe. Record pe in both cases regardless.
#
# REQUIREMENTS
#   Core11_idealgas_mod_v4.dat (Hdg lives there).
#   Replace the DATABASE path below.

DATABASE /path/to/Core11_idealgas_mod_v4.dat

SELECTED_OUTPUT 1
    -reset false
    -high_precision true
USER_PUNCH 1
    -headings case pH pe tot_C tot_Hdg m_CH4 m_CO2 m_HCO3 m_CO3 C_ratio_log
    -start
    10 PUNCH PARM(1)
    20 PUNCH -LA("H+"), -LA("e-")
    30 PUNCH TOTMOLE("C"), TOTMOLE("Hdg")
    40 PUNCH MOL("CH4"), MOL("CO2"), MOL("HCO3-"), MOL("CO3-2")
    50 rat = -99
    60 IF MOL("CO2") > 0 THEN rat = LOG10(MOL("CH4") / MOL("CO2"))
    70 PUNCH rat
    -end

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CASE 1: COUPLED H2. Reducing solution, H2 entered as normal coupled H(0).
#   Add oxidised carbon (as HCO3-, i.e. C at +4) and see where it goes.
#   Expect: nearly all of it becomes CH4. This reproduces the artefact.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
SOLUTION 1 coupled-H2 reducing
    temp      1.45
    pH        11.5
    units     mol/kgw
    Na        0.1
    Cl        0.1 charge
    C         1e-3            # oxidised carbon, 1 mmol/kgw
    H(0)      1e-3            # dissolved H2, COUPLED -- can donate electrons
    water     1
USER_PUNCH 1; -start; 10 PUNCH 1; 20 PUNCH -LA("H+"), -LA("e-"); 30 PUNCH TOTMOLE("C"), TOTMOLE("Hdg"); 40 PUNCH MOL("CH4"), MOL("CO2"), MOL("HCO3-"), MOL("CO3-2"); 50 rat = -99; 60 IF MOL("CO2") > 0 THEN rat = LOG10(MOL("CH4") / MOL("CO2")); 70 PUNCH rat; -end
END

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CASE 2: DECOUPLED H2. Identical in every other respect -- same T, pH, Na,
#   Cl, same total carbon, same H2 inventory -- but H2 entered as Hdg.
#   Expect (if the hypothesis holds): carbon stays as HCO3-/CO3-2, CH4 stays
#   negligible, and pe is set by something other than H2.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
SOLUTION 2 decoupled-H2 reducing
    temp      1.45
    pH        11.5
    units     mol/kgw
    Na        0.1
    Cl        0.1 charge
    C         1e-3            # same oxidised carbon
    Hdg       1e-3            # same H2 inventory, DECOUPLED -- inert electron-wise
    water     1
USER_PUNCH 1; -start; 10 PUNCH 2; 20 PUNCH -LA("H+"), -LA("e-"); 30 PUNCH TOTMOLE("C"), TOTMOLE("Hdg"); 40 PUNCH MOL("CH4"), MOL("CO2"), MOL("HCO3-"), MOL("CO3-2"); 50 rat = -99; 60 IF MOL("CO2") > 0 THEN rat = LOG10(MOL("CH4") / MOL("CO2")); 70 PUNCH rat; -end
END

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CASE 3 and 4: THE REALISTIC VERSION -- with a reducing mineral assemblage.
#   Cases 1-2 isolate H2 alone. But in ENTICES, Fe(II) minerals are present
#   and are the OTHER electron source named in PROGRESS Part 2b. If decoupling
#   H2 only works in the absence of minerals, that is important to know before
#   relying on it.
#
#   Uses a minimal Fe(II)-bearing assemblage. Substitute the actual ENTICES
#   secondary-phase list if a closer match is wanted, but keep it small --
#   the point is to isolate the mechanism, not to reproduce a full run.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
USE solution 1
EQUILIBRIUM_PHASES 1
    Magnetite   0 0
    Pyrrhotite  0 0
    Fe(OH)2     0 0
USER_PUNCH 1; -start; 10 PUNCH 3; 20 PUNCH -LA("H+"), -LA("e-"); 30 PUNCH TOTMOLE("C"), TOTMOLE("Hdg"); 40 PUNCH MOL("CH4"), MOL("CO2"), MOL("HCO3-"), MOL("CO3-2"); 50 rat = -99; 60 IF MOL("CO2") > 0 THEN rat = LOG10(MOL("CH4") / MOL("CO2")); 70 PUNCH rat; -end
END

USE solution 2
EQUILIBRIUM_PHASES 2
    Magnetite   0 0
    Pyrrhotite  0 0
    Fe(OH)2     0 0
USER_PUNCH 1; -start; 10 PUNCH 4; 20 PUNCH -LA("H+"), -LA("e-"); 30 PUNCH TOTMOLE("C"), TOTMOLE("Hdg"); 40 PUNCH MOL("CH4"), MOL("CO2"), MOL("HCO3-"), MOL("CO3-2"); 50 rat = -99; 60 IF MOL("CO2") > 0 THEN rat = LOG10(MOL("CH4") / MOL("CO2")); 70 PUNCH rat; -end
END

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CASE 5: CARBONATE-MINERAL CHECK (the caveat in OPTION3_decision.md sec 2).
#   Decoupled carbon would stop seeing calcite. We are NOT decoupling carbon
#   in the proposed design -- but confirm that with Hdg in play, carbon STILL
#   participates in calcite saturation. If it does, the design's main selling
#   point over a decoupled-carbon species is confirmed.
#   Expect: Calcite SI is computed and finite, and Ca is drawn down if
#   supersaturated.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
SOLUTION 3 decoupled-H2 with Ca
    temp      1.45
    pH        11.5
    units     mol/kgw
    Na        0.1
    Cl        0.1 charge
    Ca        1e-3
    C         1e-3
    Hdg       1e-3
    water     1
EQUILIBRIUM_PHASES 3
    Calcite     0 0
SELECTED_OUTPUT 2
    -reset false
    -si Calcite
    -totals Ca C
    -molalities CH4 CO2 HCO3- CO3-2
END

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# REPORTING
#   Record for every case: pe, m_CH4, m_CO2, m_HCO3-, and log10(CH4/CO2).
#   The single number that decides the design is log10(CH4/CO2) in case 2 and
#   case 4 versus case 1 and case 3.
#
#   Also record any PHREEQC warnings verbatim. A charge-balance or
#   convergence warning in the Hdg cases is itself a finding -- decoupling H2
#   changes how the proton/electron balance closes, and it may need
#   -pe or an explicit redox couple to converge cleanly.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
